<p>India’s Prime Minister Narendra Modi arrived in Israel on February 25 for a two-day visit focused on deepening India–Israel ties in defence, technology, agriculture and trade. His schedule includes talks with Prime Minister Benjamin Netanyahu and a meeting with President Isaac Herzog. Modi is also set to address the Knesset — described in Indian coverage as a first for an Indian prime minister — a high-profile appearance both sides present as a sign of the relationship’s growing strategic weight. The visit comes amid a tense regional backdrop. Reuters reporting cited heightened concern about the risk of military conflict tied to U.S.–Iran tensions, alongside continued diplomatic fallout from the Israel–Hamas war in Gaza. The trip has also drawn political scrutiny: Indian opposition figures have urged Modi to address civilian deaths in Gaza in his public remarks, while Israeli opposition leaders have debated whether to attend his Knesset address amid a protocol dispute involving the country’s Supreme Court president. Ahead of Modi’s arrival, the Knesset was lit in India’s tricolour — a public welcome gesture widely highlighted by Indian media.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/AwSGGnm6Y7Qd2gC4ADO_dbNaY3ztzD0V-XS-yFjiEXuHY2xrvxGdH5fOU4DH6E-filhRAhvGW8WcUbqa3jLak8NQBRQV9obCfDnoPSDHHrKM' alt='Indian Prime Minister Narendra Modi and Israeli Prime Minister Benjamin Netanyahu ahead of a meeting (file photo).' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Historic milestone: Indian coverage and The Hindu said Modi is set to become the first Indian prime minister to address Israel’s parliament, adding symbolism that goes well beyond routine bilateral meetings.</li><li>Symbolic welcome: Ahead of the visit, the Knesset was lit in India’s tricolour — a gesture of welcome highlighted by Indian media.</li><li>Longer arc of engagement: Firstpost traced Modi’s engagement with Israel to a 2006 study tour as Gujarat chief minister, before his first visit as prime minister in 2017 and this return trip in 2026.</li><li>Balancing diplomacy: Reporting and explainers describe India’s push to expand practical cooperation with Israel while also balancing wider Middle East interests, including its positioning on Palestine.</li><li>How Israel is framing ties: Israel’s envoy to India said the visit underscores growing warmth with Netanyahu and that the countries share a vision centred on “innovation, growth and resilience”.</li></ul><blockquote>Our nations share a robust and multifaceted strategic partnership. - Narendra Modi</blockquote><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Narendra Modi (India’s prime minister): Modi said the visit will focus on “discuss ways to strengthen co-operation” with Netanyahu and on meeting Israel’s leadership as ties expand across multiple sectors. (<a href='https://www.dawn.com/news/1975656/indias-modi-heads-to-israel-to-strengthen-cooperation'>Dawn</a>)</li><li>Israel’s opposition leadership: Israel’s opposition said it may skip the Knesset address unless Supreme Court President Yitzhak Amit is invited, as required by protocol, and asked Netanyahu to enable opposition participation. (<a href='https://www.thehindu.com/news/international/israels-lop-requests-netanyahu-to-facilitate-oppositions-participation-in-modis-parliamentary-address/article70673878.ece'>The Hindu</a>)</li><li>Priyanka Gandhi (India’s Congress party): Gandhi urged Modi to use the Knesset platform to speak about civilian deaths in Gaza. (<a href='https://www.dawn.com/news/1975656/indias-modi-heads-to-israel-to-strengthen-cooperation'>Dawn</a>)</li><li>Israel’s envoy to India: Israel’s envoy said the visit underscores growing warmth with Netanyahu and portrayed India and Israel as sharing a vision focused on “innovation, growth and resilience.” (<a href='https://www.ndtv.com/world-news/pm-modis-israel-visit-underscores-new-bonhomie-with-netanyahu-israeli-envoy-fleur-hassan-nahoum-pm-modi-in-israel-11133200#publisher=newsstand'>NDTV</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul>